WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:07.162
Laurie had just gotten home from school when Grandpa appeared, holding a tray of tea and cookies.

2
00:00:07.222 --> 00:00:08.482
"Welcome home, Laurie!

3
00:00:11.503 --> 00:00:13.263
You're just the girl I'm looking for.

4
00:00:13.423 --> 00:00:17.004
May I invite you to the study for a snack?"

5
00:00:32.396 --> 00:00:36.099
"Okay Laurie answered with a suspicious smile.

6
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.801
What was Grandpa up to now?

7
00:00:38.821 --> 00:00:43.245
"How do Japanese cookies and tea sound?"

8
00:00:43.625 --> 00:00:45.006
Grandpa asked, grinning.

9
00:00:45.026 --> 00:00:48.869
"Good." "Please follow me."

10
00:00:55.154 --> 00:00:57.937
Grandpa led the way upstairs and into the study.

11
00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:01.900
"Whoa! What's all this?"

12
00:01:02.356 --> 00:01:03.337
Laurie exclaimed.

13
00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:08.100
On the floor was a large, colorful map.

14
00:01:09.421 --> 00:01:14.305
Small buildings, ships, bridges, and figurines were scattered around it.

15
00:01:14.325 --> 00:01:20.770
"I need to attach all these bits and pieces to this map by tomorrow!

16
00:01:21.591 --> 00:01:22.391
Can you help me?"

17
00:01:22.411 --> 00:01:25.253
"Sure!" Laurie replied.

18
00:01:25.273 --> 00:01:28.196
"What's it for?"

19
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:35.842
"I'm presenting a unit at the high school on the social order of Japan during the Tokugawa period—

20
00:01:36.222 --> 00:01:43.284
from 1600 to 1867—when Japan was ruled by the powerful Tokugawa family.

21
00:01:44.864 --> 00:01:49.205
I'm going to use this map of Japan to show where things took place."

22
00:01:50.006 --> 00:01:52.306
Grandpa picked up a small wooden bridge.

23
00:01:53.286 --> 00:01:59.708
"For example, we'll use this to mark the capital, Edo, which is now called Tokyo.

24
00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:07.570
Edo was a busy place; merchants, craftsmen, and lots of samurai crossed over this bridge.

25
00:02:08.751 --> 00:02:11.211
So we'll need to put those figures around here."

26
00:02:11.231 --> 00:02:15.452
"Samurai were warriors," Laurie stated.

27
00:02:15.472 --> 00:02:17.773
"Why were there so many of them?"

28
00:02:17.793 --> 00:02:24.055
"Because the shogun—the top leader in Japan at the time—lived in Edo.

29
00:02:25.015 --> 00:02:28.456
He was a warlord who held even more power than the emperor.

30
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.804
This is his castle, actually," Grandpa said, picking up a castle replica.

31
00:02:34.824 --> 00:02:43.431
"The shogun required that samurai lords from all across Japan live in Edo for part of every year.

32
00:02:44.712 --> 00:02:47.194
That was his way of keeping an eye on them."

33
00:02:48.695 --> 00:02:50.396
"This is a samurai, right?"

34
00:02:50.796 --> 00:02:51.597
Laurie asked.

35
00:02:51.917 --> 00:02:53.879
She held up a man carrying a sword.

36
00:02:53.899 --> 00:03:01.635
"Yep. Samurai could easily be recognized by their armor, swords, and hairstyles.

37
00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:06.058
This one was probably a middle-ranking samurai.

38
00:03:07.118 --> 00:03:11.721
He would have been employed by one of the samurai lords, who were landowners.

39
00:03:12.841 --> 00:03:17.364
For centuries there was no central government to keep Japan stable.

40
00:03:19.045 --> 00:03:24.508
So there were constant civil wars between lords to expand their land and power.

41
00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:30.644
Samurai were not only highly respected, they were considered necessary!

42
00:03:31.805 --> 00:03:37.649
The warrior samurai and their lords made up the highest social class in Japan back then."

43
00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:43.173
Laurie picked up a figure of a skinny man wearing a triangular straw hat.

44
00:03:43.794 --> 00:03:45.095
"Where does this one go?"

45
00:03:45.115 --> 00:03:48.017
"He's a rice farmer.

46
00:03:49.138 --> 00:03:51.940
We'll put him in the farmlands surrounding Edo.

47
00:03:52.944 --> 00:03:55.265
Farmers were in the next highest class.

48
00:03:56.365 --> 00:04:00.347
They were important because they took care of the crops that fed the nation.

49
00:04:01.648 --> 00:04:06.470
Rice was, and still is, the main food eaten by the Japanese.

50
00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:13.692
During the Tokugawa period, rice was so valuable that it was used to pay rent!

51
00:04:13.712 --> 00:04:16.734
"This is a craftsman.

52
00:04:17.834 --> 00:04:22.156
Since he's holding this small bowl, he probably makes lacquerware."

53
00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.842
Grandpa pointed to the tray he had brought up with the tea and cookies.

54
00:04:26.862 --> 00:04:30.303
"This is Japanese lacquerware.

55
00:04:30.924 --> 00:04:35.986
The Japanese carved items out of wood and then coated them with a shiny gloss.

56
00:04:37.166 --> 00:04:41.048
They perfected this art on their bowls and boxes and things.

57
00:04:42.489 --> 00:04:50.644
Anyway, this craftsman would be in the third social class, along with weavers, sword makers, and carpenters That

58
00:04:50.704 --> 00:04:53.766
guy you're holding would probably fit into the last class."

59
00:04:53.786 --> 00:04:58.548
"It looks like he's carrying lots of stuff," Laurie said.

60
00:04:58.568 --> 00:05:00.889
"He's a merchant.

61
00:05:01.749 --> 00:05:06.912
In ancient and medieval Japan, merchants were at the bottom of the social hierarchy.

62
00:05:08.212 --> 00:05:14.795
In a culture that valued hard work, making money off someone else's labor wasn't as respectable as doing

63
00:05:14.835 --> 00:05:15.776
the labor yourself.

64
00:05:17.068 --> 00:05:23.634
But people began realizing that merchants were a key part of the economy, and merchants' value grew.

65
00:05:24.855 --> 00:05:28.418
Without them, cities might not have developed so quickly."

66
00:05:29.518 --> 00:05:33.762
Laurie nodded as she added a cherry tree near the shogun's castle.

67
00:05:33.782 --> 00:05:40.828
"This medieval Japanese society had a rigid class system, but it was also peaceful.

68
00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:48.703
The civil wars ended when the Tokugawa family claimed control over all the other warlords.

69
00:05:50.083 --> 00:05:58.887
Big cities developed and prospered; universities were built for high-ranking samurai; and arts like poetry, literature, painting, and

70
00:05:58.967 --> 00:06:01.928
lacquerware expressed Japanese culture.

71
00:06:03.468 --> 00:06:10.272
The Tokugawa shogun thought the best way to preserve this peace and stability was to close Japan off

72
00:06:10.372 --> 00:06:18.316
from other countries, particularly Western countries, which had a history of creating Christian empires and trading colonies.

73
00:06:19.597 --> 00:06:22.258
So foreigners weren't usually allowed in Japan."

74
00:06:22.278 --> 00:06:24.880
"Then who's this guy?

75
00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:26.961
He doesn't look Japanese!"

76
00:06:27.401 --> 00:06:31.383
Laurie exclaimed, picking up a white man dressed in a military uniform.

77
00:06:31.403 --> 00:06:34.020
"Ha! You found him!

78
00:06:34.821 --> 00:06:37.682
That's Commodore Perry," Grandpa said.

79
00:06:37.702 --> 00:06:47.746
"Despite Japan's wishes, the United States really wanted Japan to open its ports to their traders and sailors.

80
00:06:47.926 --> 00:06:53.468
In 1853 they sent Perry over with four armed, black naval ships.

81
00:06:54.669 --> 00:06:58.871
They warned the Japanese that if ports weren't opened, they would fire!"

82
00:06:58.891 --> 00:07:02.092
"What did the Japanese do then?"

83
00:07:03.328 --> 00:07:10.493
"Intimidated by the guns and steamships—which were new to the Japanese— they signed an agreement with the U.S.

84
00:07:11.774 --> 00:07:15.136
After that things changed rapidly.

85
00:07:15.316 --> 00:07:19.979
In 1867 the shogun resigned, and the emperor suddenly died.

86
00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:24.282
Then a young emperor called Meiji came to power.

87
00:07:25.403 --> 00:07:31.887
Emperor Meiji realized that if Japan wanted to keep itself safe in the modern world, it would need

88
00:07:32.007 --> 00:07:33.128
Western technology.

89
00:07:34.232 --> 00:07:40.715
So the Japanese imported ideas about education, politics, and science from the United States and Europe.

90
00:07:42.536 --> 00:07:45.597
But this map only covers the Tokugawa era.

91
00:07:46.457 --> 00:07:48.318
What do you say—are we finished?"

92
00:07:49.518 --> 00:07:53.300
Laurie scanned the map, now speckled with people and buildings.

93
00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.401
"I think so," she replied.

94
00:07:56.421 --> 00:08:03.457
"Tokugawa Japan looks like it might have been an interesting place to visit except I wouldn't have been

95
00:08:03.497 --> 00:08:04.937
allowed to enter the country!"

96
00:08:06.218 --> 00:08:07.159
Grandpa chuckled.

97
00:08:07.179 --> 00:08:11.621
"You're absolutely right.

98
00:08:12.722 --> 00:08:14.202
Well, thank you, Laurie.

99
00:08:15.363 --> 00:08:16.844
I couldn't have done it without you.

100
00:08:17.904 --> 00:08:19.505
Now how about our snack?"

101
00:08:19.925 --> 00:08:20.406
he said.

102
00:08:20.426 --> 00:08:26.789
"In Japan, before you begin eating, you say itadakimasu!

103
00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:29.311
So on the count of three, ready?

104
00:08:29.771 --> 00:08:33.651
One, two, three—" "Itadakimasu!"

105
00:08:34.032 --> 00:08:36.317
they both cried and reached for their cookies.
